    "result_text": "All 64 tests pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned the identity key and reworked duplicate detection to satisfy the specification.\n\n**New identity key** \u2014 now `SHA-256 hex` over `normalizedTelephone + '|' + lowerEmail + '|' + soundex(lastName)` (previously a plain `telephone|email|householdId` string). I extracted this into a shared `IdentityKeys` utility (`src/main/java/.../mapper/IdentityKeys.java`) with a standard American Soundex implementation, so the mapper (DTO's `identityKey` field) and the controller's duplicate check compute the exact same value. The helpers had to live in a plain class rather than as `default`/`static` methods on the `OwnerMapper` interface, because MapStruct was treating the `String\u2192String` helpers as candidate property-mapping methods.\n\n**Duplicate detection (409)** \u2014 still the single identity key, still ignoring soft-deleted owners, and the email-domain blocklist still applies first (during email normalization). Because telephone is part of the key, two owners with the same last name + postcode but different telephones now produce different keys and are no longer a hard duplicate.\n\n**Removed the household-duplicate 409 block** \u2014 the separate `DuplicateOwnerHouseholdException` block driven by the computed `householdId` is gone (import removed). `householdId` is still computed and retained purely for household grouping (size, membership-level cap, points). The exception class and its advice handler remain but are simply never thrown now.\n\n**Soft-match** \u2014 `findPossibleDuplicate` now flags an owner when its `identityKey` differs but `soundex(lastName)` **and** postcode match an existing (non-deleted) owner \u2014 so same-last-name/postcode/different-phone owners are created and flagged `possibleDuplicate=true` instead of rejected. Declared `sharesHousehold` members stay exempt.\n\nVerified behaviour matches the acceptance tests: `Cp52`/`Cp28` (64-hex key + collision\u2192409), `Cp35` (soundex+postcode soft match), `Cp10` (same name+postcode, different phone \u2192 soft match not 409), and the household grouping tests (`Cp11`, `Cp23`, `Cp36`, `Cp39`, `Cp51`).",
